# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cador-2-multidocument-runtime — N documents, isolated engines, shared blackboard, cooperative coroutine scheduler
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: cador-1-split-nihil-cador
#+BRANCH: cador-multidocument

* Context

Sub-task 2 of the =cador-multidocument-host= epic (§5, §6 C1–C8, and the
analysis document's §4.2 invariant "one runner, N parked contexts").
Developed on its OWN branch (=cador-multidocument=) in parallel with
sub-task 1, per the owner.

* Problem

cador must present a multi-document world to AutoLISP, matching AutoCAD /
BricsCAD MDI semantics, on a single-runner cooperative model — without a
global Lisp environment (C1) and without concurrent per-document
execution (C2).

* Proposal (owner's design, to be specified then implemented)

- **N document images.** Each open drawing is a document with its OWN
  AutoLISP namespace: separate value/function cells, a separate AutoLISP
  engine (evaluation context), and a distinct loaded Lisp program set.
  Switching document switches the active namespace + the active drawing.
- **Shared blackboard, one heap.** A single AutoLISP heap is kept for the
  whole session; the blackboard (=vl-bb-ref= / =vl-bb-set=) is shared
  across documents WITHOUT copying values between per-document heaps —
  there is one heap, so blackboard values are shared by identity, not
  duplicated (values only, never functions — C3).
- **Per-document drawing.** Each namespace owns its drawing (the cador
  entity DB); entity handles / selection sets / ldata are
  document-tagged, cross-document dereference signals (C5).
- **Scheduler = cooperative coroutines.** One thread per document, but run
  as a coroutine (the aldo signal/park handoff model): at most one
  evaluation runs at any instant, any number parked (C2, C8 — no worker
  thread is *required*, but the per-document thread is the coroutine
  carrier). One aldo (debugger) instance per document ⇒ ~2 threads per
  document total (the document runner + its aldo), used cooperatively.
- **Park/resume points.** Reuse the debugger's poll-point / signal-park
  machinery; specify where a document parks and what resuming restores;
  a host call outstanding at a park is the hazard to handle (epic §1.2b).

* Non-negotiable (from the epic §6)

C1 no global env; C2 no concurrent per-document execution; C3 blackboard
values-only; C5 document-tagged handles; C8 single-runner, no mandatory
worker thread.

* Acceptance criteria

- [ ] N documents open concurrently, each with an isolated namespace +
      engine + loaded program set + drawing.
- [ ] Blackboard values shared across documents via the one shared heap,
      no copying; functions rejected.
- [ ] Cooperative scheduler: exactly one running context, others parked;
      document switch parks the current and resumes the target.
- [ ] One aldo instance per document; the coroutine handoff mirrors the
      same-image two-thread debugger design.
- [ ] Cross-document handle dereference signals; per-document drawing
      isolation holds.
- [ ] Conformance probes (E1 symbol identity, E2 blackboard sharing) run
      against cador.

* Notes

Depends on cador-1 (the host must be named cador and own the entity DB
first) and on the D1/D2 specification (cador-3) for the formal
autolisp↔host protocol the scheduler and namespace model realise.
